    "options": {},
    "statements": [],
    "parts": [
      {
        "id": "i",
        "text": "Which claim correctly explains why rocks, soil, minerals, and varied landforms support life rather than acting as lifeless background?",
        "marks": 1,
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "text": "Rocks break down with plant and animal remains into soil that holds nutrients such as nitrogen and potassium, and varied landforms create different habitats."
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "text": "Rocks, soil, and minerals are only a hard stage under living things and never supply materials life needs."
          },
          {
            "id": "C",
            "text": "Soil can feed plants even if rocks never break down and plant or animal remains never mix in."
          },
          {
            "id": "D",
            "text": "Plant growth and habitats appear first, and only afterward do rocks break down into nutrient-rich soil."
          }
        ]
      },
